rule in PLAYBOOK methodology.

## Parallel work

Same-repo parallel *committing* sessions need a worktree (different repos: none — separate
`.git/` already isolates them). One strong prompt on the **primary** is the DEFAULT; a parallel
committing session is the exception, and it launches only when **ALL FOUR** of [#441]'s
conditions are YES — **(1)** two substantial independent BUILD arcs (not one goal split, not a
build plus a doc ride-along, not tiny disjoint edits); **(2)** zero shared gate-forced surfaces
(JOURNAL, BACKLOG/`tasks/` rows + regen, generated indexes), or a written contract pre-resolves
them — two lanes on the same substantive file is always a NO; **(3)** wall-clock genuinely
matters; **(4)** the operator takes the serial gate. **One NO = fat prompt** — no partial
credit. This **replaces** the superseded three-check test ("disjoint substantive files? two
distinct goals? more than a single-file edit?"), which mapped onto conditions 1–2 and had no
equivalent of 3–4; the corpus carries one launch test, at PLAYBOOK Ch8 §0. The canonical safe
pair is one code item ∥ one doc item. To start: keep stream A in the
**primary**, `claude --worktree <B>` for stream B, integrate **from the primary** (`/ship` A,
then `git merge --no-ff worktree-<B>` + push + teardown). Shared canonical files are handled,
not parallelized (BACKLOG removal via the closure loop; JOURNAL = trivial double-prepend;
operator is the serial gate). Full rule — START recipe, `.worktreeinclude`, `/ship`-from-
primary, no-leftovers teardown — in PLAYBOOK "Parallel sessions & worktree discipline."

**Batch shape (N lanes).** When the work is a batch rather than a pair — ONE plan → N
file-disjoint lanes → ONE serial integrator — the protocol is PLAYBOOK "The batch protocol —
ONE plan → N lanes → ONE integrator", with `/lane-boot` and `/lane-integrate` as its two
commands. A successor bundle points at that section; it does not restate it (ADR-110 §1 item 3).

